WEBVTT

1
00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.202
All life
began in the deep blue sea.

2
00:00:43.544 --> 00:00:45.337
Its flow and force...

3
00:00:48.131 --> 00:00:49.883
...have shaped our world.

4
00:00:55.472 --> 00:01:00.018
Revered, and feared...

5
00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:03.981
...since humans first
walked the Earth,

6
00:01:08.652 --> 00:01:11.446
an immense hidden realm,

7
00:01:14.241 --> 00:01:17.119
visible only in our imagination,

8
00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:24.001
for hundreds
of thousands of years.

9
00:01:29.131 --> 00:01:33.719
Only now, are we discovering
what our ocean means...

10
00:01:37.681 --> 00:01:39.433
...for our world.

11
00:01:47.399 --> 00:01:51.278
What we have found
could change the course of

12
00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:54.114
our future, forever.

13
00:02:40.619 --> 00:02:45.207
Humans have always looked
out at the open blue horizon

14
00:02:45.374 --> 00:02:48.001
and wondered what lies beyond.

15
00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:56.510
Yet it has remained
almost entirely a mystery.

16
00:03:03.809 --> 00:03:07.187
After living for nearly
100 years on this planet,

17
00:03:07.521 --> 00:03:11.608
I now understand the most
important place on Earth is not

18
00:03:11.692 --> 00:03:14.194
on land, but at sea.

19
00:03:14.986 --> 00:03:19.700
And today we're living in the
greatest age of ocean discovery.

20
00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:24.538
For many of us the world
beyond the beach is still dark,

21
00:03:24.621 --> 00:03:28.291
threatening, and dangerous...
out of sight and most certainly

22
00:03:28.375 --> 00:03:29.835
out of mind.

23
00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:34.756
It's time to take the plunge,
to go beneath the waves.

24
00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:37.759
For once you've
truly seen the sea,

25
00:03:37.843 --> 00:03:40.762
you'll never look at Earth
in the same way again.

26
00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:46.268
In my lifetime,

27
00:03:46.351 --> 00:03:50.272
our ability to see the
ocean has been transformed.

28
00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:57.362
The invention of scuba
gear changed everything.

29
00:03:59.364 --> 00:04:02.784
Suddenly, an extraordinary
new world of wonder and

30
00:04:02.868 --> 00:04:04.536
surprise came into view.

31
00:04:10.125 --> 00:04:13.587
It wasn't long before we found
a way to explore that

32
00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.839
dark distant part of the planet.

33
00:04:17.132 --> 00:04:19.885
That had remained
hidden for so long.

34
00:04:22.679 --> 00:04:24.765
We journeyed into the deep...

35
00:04:27.184 --> 00:04:30.479
...and gazed at
the bottom of the sea.

36
00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.486
In the years that followed I
could never have imagined what

37
00:04:37.569 --> 00:04:41.948
we'd find, miles
below the surface.

38
00:04:44.993 --> 00:04:50.123
Yet, to this day, we have
seen more of other planets

39
00:04:50.207 --> 00:04:52.375
than we have of the ocean.

40
00:04:54.211 --> 00:04:58.173
Now we are making discoveries
that completely change our

41
00:04:58.256 --> 00:05:01.885
understanding and
could offer a better future

42
00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:03.929
for everyone on Earth,

43
00:05:08.433 --> 00:05:11.269
even in the waters
we know least about,

44
00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:13.980
those furthest from land.

45
00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:19.069
The high seas or open ocean.

46
00:05:20.946 --> 00:05:23.448
The last great wilderness.

47
00:05:28.578 --> 00:05:30.831
It's not owned by any nation,

48
00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:34.042
but shared by
everyone in the world.

49
00:05:34.918 --> 00:05:37.879
The open ocean is truly ours.

50
00:05:38.213 --> 00:05:41.925
Its waters are the deepest
and the most remote.

51
00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:45.929
Much of it has still never
been seen by human beings.

52
00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:57.065
It's our final frontier.

53
00:06:06.867 --> 00:06:12.163
Until recently, we assumed it
to be a largely empty desert.

54
00:06:17.127 --> 00:06:20.839
The few creatures we knew
of wandering this open world

55
00:06:20.922 --> 00:06:22.841
remained a mystery.

56
00:06:34.311 --> 00:06:37.981
Now a new, far more
connected picture,

57
00:06:38.064 --> 00:06:41.192
is emerging of the big blue.

58
00:06:46.615 --> 00:06:50.994
Some of these travelers such
as sharks and tuna have been

59
00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:53.705
tracked making
enormous migrations,

60
00:06:53.788 --> 00:07:00.253
across the entire planet, on
an endless search for food.

61
00:07:10.013 --> 00:07:13.433
And we have only just learnt
how these voyagers find

62
00:07:13.516 --> 00:07:15.268
their way out here,

63
00:07:17.687 --> 00:07:21.983
steering along the twists and
turns of particular currents.

64
00:07:30.408 --> 00:07:33.411
They come together
from across the globe,

65
00:07:34.704 --> 00:07:37.290
at a few precious places.

66
00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:44.589
Seamounts.

67
00:07:46.883 --> 00:07:51.262
Submarine mountains soaring
up to three miles high.

68
00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.854
They stand in the path
of ocean currents,

69
00:08:00.605 --> 00:08:06.403
causing water to rise from
the deep, bringing with it food.

70
00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:21.751
Only now are we understanding
just how important

71
00:08:21.835 --> 00:08:23.670
they really are.

72
00:08:24.879 --> 00:08:30.510
As pit stops and endpoints
on ocean odysseys.

73
00:08:52.866 --> 00:08:56.619
Perhaps most exciting of all,
we have just discovered there

74
00:08:56.703 --> 00:09:01.416
are nearly twice as many of
these oases as we had thought.

75
00:09:02.459 --> 00:09:06.087
Some 40,000 of them
across the sea floor.

76
00:09:16.306 --> 00:09:21.227
So the open ocean is not a
featureless desert after all.

77
00:09:23.313 --> 00:09:26.816
It is more connected than
we had ever imagined.

78
00:10:01.142 --> 00:10:04.437
Back towards the shore,
closer to land,

79
00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:07.107
there is a more familiar world.

80
00:10:08.525 --> 00:10:11.820
This is the other
great ocean realm,

81
00:10:11.986 --> 00:10:14.155
the shallow coastal seas.

82
00:10:16.366 --> 00:10:19.953
And this is where we
get most of our seafood.

83
00:10:29.379 --> 00:10:33.466
The shallow seas we regard
as being owned by the nations

84
00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.760
living on the land next to them.

85
00:10:36.594 --> 00:10:39.848
These coastal countries have
exclusive rights to exploit an

86
00:10:39.931 --> 00:10:43.685
area within a few hundred
kilometers offshore.

87
00:10:47.313 --> 00:10:50.775
This shallow region above
the continental shelf,

88
00:10:51.234 --> 00:10:54.946
is a magical world
where everything is

89
00:10:55.029 --> 00:10:57.240
bathed in sunlight.

90
00:11:27.812 --> 00:11:30.315
This is giant kelp,

91
00:11:31.482 --> 00:11:34.402
the tallest
living thing in the ocean.

92
00:11:36.279 --> 00:11:38.489
Beneath its towering canopies,

93
00:11:38.573 --> 00:11:41.784
we've discovered a
high-rise community.

94
00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:56.507
The forest needs its many
residents to survive.

95
00:12:02.347 --> 00:12:07.644
Sea urchins do the gardening
and the kelp is kept in check.

96
00:12:09.687 --> 00:12:14.609
If these gardeners get too
abundant, this is the result.

97
00:12:16.694 --> 00:12:21.366
Fortunately for the kelp,
there are urchin-eaters too.

98
00:12:30.583 --> 00:12:33.628
It's a world in
delicate balance.

99
00:12:45.765 --> 00:12:47.767
To have found,
just off our coasts,

100
00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:52.146
jungles to rival any on land
was extraordinary.

101
00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:58.319
It was the equivalent of
discovering a new tropical

102
00:12:58.403 --> 00:13:02.615
rainforest full of marvels that
we had barely glimpsed before.

103
00:13:06.577 --> 00:13:10.957
Scientists had no idea of the
sheer scale of these forests,

104
00:13:11.582 --> 00:13:13.960
or the speed at which they grew.

105
00:13:16.796 --> 00:13:19.465
They border a quarter
of the world's coasts,

106
00:13:20.258 --> 00:13:23.219
and we are still finding
more of them even now.

107
00:13:33.229 --> 00:13:36.941
And only within the last couple
of years have we found the

108
00:13:37.025 --> 00:13:39.527
world's largest seagrass meadow.

109
00:13:43.156 --> 00:13:45.616
This increased the
global estimate of this

110
00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.286
habitat by nearly half.

111
00:13:54.250 --> 00:13:59.380
And we have found that ocean
jungles and meadows absorb far

112
00:13:59.464 --> 00:14:03.760
more carbon than the same
area of rainforest on land.

113
00:14:06.596 --> 00:14:09.599
We now know that
together they could help us

114
00:14:09.682 --> 00:14:12.185
avoid climate disaster.

115
00:14:22.403 --> 00:14:26.282
In the coastal seas close to
the equator lies a tropical

116
00:14:26.365 --> 00:14:30.661
world of seemingly
endless surprise.

117
00:14:35.458 --> 00:14:38.586
The first time I used scuba
gear to dive on a coral reef,

118
00:14:38.669 --> 00:14:41.881
I was so taken aback by
the spectacle before me,

119
00:14:41.964 --> 00:14:45.760
I forgot, momentarily,
to breathe!

120
00:14:59.565 --> 00:15:01.943
Nothing that I'd ever
seen on land had come close

121
00:15:02.026 --> 00:15:05.613
to the sensory overload
of so much life,

122
00:15:05.696 --> 00:15:09.075
and such diversity
right before my eyes.

123
00:15:27.885 --> 00:15:30.429
Everywhere I looked,
at every scale,

124
00:15:30.513 --> 00:15:32.390
there was something new.

125
00:15:40.481 --> 00:15:43.151
One could spend days
swimming above it and

126
00:15:43.484 --> 00:15:45.611
never tire of the colors,

127
00:15:45.862 --> 00:15:48.239
the movement,
the interactions.

128
00:15:48.489 --> 00:15:51.617
It's life at its
most mesmerizing.

129
00:15:59.375 --> 00:16:03.546
We knew that coral reefs
harbored astonishing diversity

130
00:16:03.629 --> 00:16:06.007
ever since we first
set eyes on them.

131
00:16:06.883 --> 00:16:10.052
But the reason they did
so remained a mystery.

132
00:16:20.021 --> 00:16:22.815
I remember reading how
Charles Darwin puzzled over

133
00:16:22.899 --> 00:16:27.195
how reefs form and how
they can turn even the most

134
00:16:27.278 --> 00:16:31.490
barren places
into oases of life.

135
00:16:33.951 --> 00:16:37.872
We now know their
secret and it's wonderful.

136
00:16:41.209 --> 00:16:43.711
Corals are animals.

137
00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:53.554
But this is just
part of their story.

138
00:17:05.191 --> 00:17:08.861
Within their bodies
live plant-like algae.

139
00:17:12.198 --> 00:17:16.619
The algae use sunlight to
produce the food that corals

140
00:17:16.702 --> 00:17:18.079
need to survive.

141
00:17:25.044 --> 00:17:26.754
As the corals grow,

142
00:17:27.255 --> 00:17:29.298
they build stone castles
around their

143
00:17:29.382 --> 00:17:31.467
soft bodies for protection.

144
00:17:33.886 --> 00:17:37.306
And in doing so,
they create the planet's

145
00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:39.267
largest living structures.

146
00:17:46.607 --> 00:17:50.486
It is extraordinary to think
that this one relationship can

147
00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:54.323
turn undersea deserts into
the most complex and diverse

148
00:17:54.407 --> 00:17:56.409
community in the ocean.

149
00:18:01.998 --> 00:18:03.541
And the closer one looks...

150
00:18:06.043 --> 00:18:07.962
...the more one sees.

151
00:18:12.425 --> 00:18:16.846
In this crowded world of color,
there isn't a shape,

152
00:18:17.805 --> 00:18:21.517
or size, that's not at home.

153
00:18:36.782 --> 00:18:40.745
A greater variety of life
may seem hard to imagine,

154
00:18:41.662 --> 00:18:43.706
yet we keep finding more...

155
00:18:48.586 --> 00:18:49.920
...and more.

156
00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:59.305
These little boxer crabs have
been found to wear clumps of

157
00:18:59.388 --> 00:19:04.143
venomous anemones as gloves
with which to defend themselves.

158
00:19:05.811 --> 00:19:09.940
And the peacock mantis shrimp
has the most complex eyesight

159
00:19:10.024 --> 00:19:11.567
of any animal.

160
00:19:13.361 --> 00:19:17.615
2,000 new marine species
are being discovered every year.

161
00:19:20.826 --> 00:19:23.579
From coral-crunchers
and grazers,

162
00:19:26.332 --> 00:19:27.541
to helpers,

163
00:19:30.711 --> 00:19:31.962
and hunters.

164
00:19:36.967 --> 00:19:40.429
We once believed that if
you removed the sharks,

165
00:19:40.513 --> 00:19:42.139
there would be more fish.

166
00:19:45.017 --> 00:19:48.104
Now we are learning
how wrong we were.

167
00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:54.735
We're seeing that the
entire reef depends on all

168
00:19:54.819 --> 00:19:56.487
of these relationships.

169
00:20:02.284 --> 00:20:07.206
If just one is removed,
everything and everyone that

170
00:20:07.289 --> 00:20:11.377
relies on this coral
community could be in trouble.

171
00:20:33.858 --> 00:20:36.152
There are things we have
discovered about the shallow

172
00:20:36.235 --> 00:20:40.531
coastal seas that directly
affect our everyday life.

173
00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:45.453
All eight billion of us.

174
00:20:55.129 --> 00:20:58.299
Currents sweep up the walls
of the continental shelf,

175
00:20:58.382 --> 00:21:00.968
carrying nutrients
from the ocean floor,

176
00:21:01.051 --> 00:21:05.514
so creating the most
productive waters in our ocean.

177
00:21:11.395 --> 00:21:16.942
This makes them the perfect
place for phytoplankton.

178
00:21:23.741 --> 00:21:28.954
These single-celled plants
flourish here in unimaginable

179
00:21:29.038 --> 00:21:34.835
quantities, absorbing vast
amounts of carbon dioxide.

180
00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:41.717
We have just discovered that
ocean plankton remove almost a

181
00:21:41.801 --> 00:21:44.887
third of our carbon emissions.

182
00:21:46.555 --> 00:21:51.769
They could be our greatest ally
in avoiding climate catastrophe.

183
00:21:54.897 --> 00:21:59.193
What is more, we now know that
they produce more oxygen than

184
00:21:59.276 --> 00:22:02.196
all the trees on Earth combined.

185
00:22:07.326 --> 00:22:10.412
That is half of
the air we breathe.

186
00:22:19.964 --> 00:22:23.634
They are also the most
important source of food under

187
00:22:23.717 --> 00:22:24.677
the waves.

188
00:22:29.682 --> 00:22:33.435
Yet until recently, we have
been unable to see this world

189
00:22:33.519 --> 00:22:35.104
in such detail.

190
00:22:46.907 --> 00:22:50.536
New images such as these,
reveal that billions of these

191
00:22:50.619 --> 00:22:55.082
tiny battles are taking
place at any one moment.

192
00:23:01.213 --> 00:23:06.218
These zooplankton are the most
abundant animals on Earth.

193
00:23:11.348 --> 00:23:14.268
They're fuel for the
biggest shoals of fish,

194
00:23:16.687 --> 00:23:19.690
and almost every food
chain in the sea.

195
00:24:09.031 --> 00:24:12.701
The shallow coastal seas
are truly the foundation

196
00:24:12.785 --> 00:24:14.995
of the entire ocean.

197
00:24:22.419 --> 00:24:25.255
And feed billions of us.

198
00:24:48.445 --> 00:24:53.575
This age of discovery has shown
our ocean to be more complex

199
00:24:53.659 --> 00:24:56.161
and more delicately
balanced than we could

200
00:24:56.245 --> 00:24:58.163
have ever imagined.

201
00:25:01.166 --> 00:25:05.754
It's revealed that this vast,
mysterious world is bound to us

202
00:25:05.838 --> 00:25:09.049
in ways we had never
dreamt possible.

203
00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:17.182
But to truly understand
its importance,

204
00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:20.894
we must first open our
eyes to what is happening

205
00:25:20.978 --> 00:25:22.271
right now...

206
00:25:23.313 --> 00:25:24.857
...below the waves.

207
00:25:46.045 --> 00:25:48.797
Few of us imagine this.

208
00:26:07.733 --> 00:26:08.901
From the surface,

209
00:26:08.984 --> 00:26:11.862
you would have no idea
that this is happening.

210
00:26:12.488 --> 00:26:17.951
It has remained hidden
from view... until now.

211
00:26:31.757 --> 00:26:35.219
A modern industrial
bottom-trawler scours the

212
00:26:35.302 --> 00:26:38.764
ocean floor with a
chain or metal beam,

213
00:26:39.056 --> 00:26:43.102
forcing anything it disturbs
into the net behind.

214
00:26:52.945 --> 00:26:55.572
It smashes its way
across the seabed,

215
00:26:56.907 --> 00:26:59.660
destroying nearly
everything in its path,

216
00:27:00.661 --> 00:27:04.957
often on the hunt for
just a single species.

217
00:27:14.842 --> 00:27:18.137
Almost everything
else is discarded.

218
00:27:21.348 --> 00:27:24.977
Over three-quarters
of a trawler's catch may

219
00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:26.895
be thrown away.

220
00:27:29.231 --> 00:27:33.193
It's hard to imagine a more
wasteful way to catch fish.

221
00:27:43.745 --> 00:27:47.791
An area almost the size of
the entire Amazon rainforest

222
00:27:47.875 --> 00:27:50.294
is trawled every year.

223
00:27:52.087 --> 00:27:55.716
And much of that seabed
is plowed again.

224
00:27:56.341 --> 00:27:58.343
Over, and over.

225
00:28:07.769 --> 00:28:11.899
This churning of the
sediment unleashes vast

226
00:28:11.982 --> 00:28:14.359
amounts of carbon dioxide,

227
00:28:17.195 --> 00:28:21.283
which in turn contributes
to the warming of our planet.

228
00:28:31.501 --> 00:28:35.464
The trawlers tear the seabed
with such force that their

229
00:28:35.547 --> 00:28:39.343
trails of destruction
can be seen from space.

230
00:29:00.364 --> 00:29:03.575
Very few places
are safe from this.

231
00:29:05.661 --> 00:29:09.915
Including almost nowhere
in my own country.

232
00:29:25.639 --> 00:29:29.935
We have lived
and relied on food from the sea

233
00:29:30.018 --> 00:29:33.730
around the island
for over 5,000 years.

234
00:29:36.483 --> 00:29:39.069
Everything is so
different under the sea.

235
00:29:41.822 --> 00:29:44.908
There's no houses, no roads,
no telephones.

236
00:29:47.536 --> 00:29:51.331
It's just nature, 100%.

237
00:30:01.842 --> 00:30:04.094
When I used to go
scallop diving,

238
00:30:04.177 --> 00:30:06.763
you would dive
down to the sea bed and

239
00:30:06.847 --> 00:30:09.057
just acclimatize yourself.

240
00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:23.530
Things that look like plants
down there are animals,

241
00:30:28.035 --> 00:30:31.163
and they've been there
for millions of years.

242
00:30:40.297 --> 00:30:45.177
Slowly but surely, you would
see a complete bed of scallops.

243
00:30:50.599 --> 00:30:54.519
It's ancient, can take
100 years to grow.

244
00:31:29.888 --> 00:31:34.184
The first time that I dived
over an area that a scallop

245
00:31:34.267 --> 00:31:39.856
dredger had just been over,
it was just heartbreaking.

246
00:31:47.030 --> 00:31:50.117
All sorts of animals
just smashed to pieces.

247
00:31:51.535 --> 00:31:56.706
It was like swimming
over the Garden of Eden to

248
00:31:56.790 --> 00:31:58.667
a nuclear winter.

249
00:32:04.464 --> 00:32:10.303
The community watched from
the shore as these boats go

250
00:32:10.387 --> 00:32:14.683
up and down, closer, and closer,

251
00:32:15.851 --> 00:32:22.524
over and over again,
just for a few scallops.

252
00:32:31.658 --> 00:32:35.370
The scallop dredgers said,
"We have the right to do this,

253
00:32:35.745 --> 00:32:37.664
and it's completely legal."

254
00:32:44.296 --> 00:32:49.843
They were taking the
future out of the sea,

255
00:32:50.093 --> 00:32:54.431
and the island community
would be left with the wreckage.

256
00:32:58.727 --> 00:33:04.107
A feeling was just of
complete disbelief.

257
00:33:07.819 --> 00:33:10.947
How can this be
allowed to happen?

258
00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:25.754
It's difficult to try and
explain to people just exactly

259
00:33:25.837 --> 00:33:28.131
how abundant it once was here.

260
00:33:30.508 --> 00:33:32.844
Just how much has been lost?

261
00:33:42.020 --> 00:33:45.023
-The idea of
bulldozing through a pristine,

262
00:33:45.106 --> 00:33:47.192
rainforest causes outrage,

263
00:33:47.901 --> 00:33:50.528
yet we do the equivalent
underwater thousands

264
00:33:50.612 --> 00:33:53.073
of times every day.

265
00:33:56.993 --> 00:34:02.374
Ancient seagrass meadows,
plowed into silt.

266
00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:14.302
Delicate, 100-year-old
sponge gardens,

267
00:34:14.928 --> 00:34:17.847
that once sheltered
bustling communities,

268
00:34:23.853 --> 00:34:25.981
destroyed in an instant.

269
00:34:54.259 --> 00:34:58.054
Surely, you would assume
it must be illegal?

270
00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:02.517
But it is not only legal,
but actually encouraged by

271
00:35:02.601 --> 00:35:04.019
the laws of today.

272
00:35:05.604 --> 00:35:08.898
Bottom trawling is still
allowed in many so-called

273
00:35:08.982 --> 00:35:12.152
Marine Protected
Areas worldwide.

274
00:35:14.029 --> 00:35:17.157
And perhaps even
more astonishingly,

275
00:35:17.282 --> 00:35:19.701
it is subsidized by governments.

276
00:35:20.827 --> 00:35:26.166
$20 billion are spent every
year supporting overfishing

277
00:35:26.249 --> 00:35:28.418
on an industrial scale.

278
00:36:13.046 --> 00:36:16.132
Vast factories now
travel the seas.

279
00:36:21.096 --> 00:36:24.766
They work day and night.

280
00:36:27.227 --> 00:36:31.606
Further and faster than ever.

281
00:36:33.692 --> 00:36:36.486
It seems nowhere is off-limits.

282
00:36:42.742 --> 00:36:44.828
Even the open ocean.

283
00:37:10.562 --> 00:37:15.900
Lines of baited hooks
50 miles long reel in

284
00:37:15.984 --> 00:37:19.028
millions of sharks every year.

285
00:37:29.539 --> 00:37:34.961
We have now killed two-thirds
of all large predatory fish.

286
00:37:41.718 --> 00:37:44.846
Walls of nets hundreds
of meters high,

287
00:37:45.930 --> 00:37:47.932
leave few survivors.

288
00:38:00.028 --> 00:38:04.866
Sharks and turtles survived
the extinction of the dinosaurs

289
00:38:05.450 --> 00:38:07.827
but may not outlive this.

290
00:38:14.167 --> 00:38:16.044
The change is simple.

291
00:38:16.336 --> 00:38:20.173
We once fished a few
places near shore to

292
00:38:20.256 --> 00:38:22.050
feed our communities.

293
00:38:22.133 --> 00:38:26.554
Now we fish everywhere,
all the time.

294
00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:29.641
And I have lived
through this change.

295
00:38:35.355 --> 00:38:37.982
There are tales
from 400 years ago,

296
00:38:38.441 --> 00:38:39.651
of coastal waters,

297
00:38:39.734 --> 00:38:43.822
"so thick with fish, boats could
barely move through them."

298
00:38:51.579 --> 00:38:56.417
No-one alive today has
known the abundance of a

299
00:38:56.501 --> 00:38:58.211
truly wild ocean.

300
00:39:04.384 --> 00:39:08.513
There are now simply
fewer fish in the sea.

301
00:39:14.352 --> 00:39:18.398
Wherever we look, in
every part of our ocean,

302
00:39:18.481 --> 00:39:20.483
the evidence is clear.

303
00:39:27.991 --> 00:39:32.370
Many sea bird colonies are
starving and in the final

304
00:39:32.453 --> 00:39:34.163
stages of collapse.

305
00:39:39.294 --> 00:39:42.964
Every year more nests fail.

306
00:39:48.761 --> 00:39:53.850
It's the same story of change
in every ocean of the world.

307
00:40:13.828 --> 00:40:16.456
Before, we
were working in harmony

308
00:40:16.539 --> 00:40:17.707
with the ocean.

309
00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:05.171
Then everything changed.

310
00:41:43.501 --> 00:41:45.336
Where the trawlers
fish is where we fish.

311
00:41:51.801 --> 00:41:54.303
We see them closer to shore.

312
00:42:12.864 --> 00:42:14.490
Our nets used to be full.

313
00:42:17.577 --> 00:42:20.621
We see different, different,
different types of fish.

314
00:42:24.459 --> 00:42:25.793
Today?

315
00:42:26.461 --> 00:42:27.795
No.

316
00:42:34.802 --> 00:42:38.556
The bigger size are gone.
They are no more.

317
00:42:45.021 --> 00:42:47.023
Even the smallest,
the smallest ones,

318
00:42:47.106 --> 00:42:49.484
we are after them
because there's no fish.

319
00:42:54.822 --> 00:42:59.827
These days, when I throw,
I catch plastic.

320
00:43:00.369 --> 00:43:03.247
To even throw a line,
your hook and line,

321
00:43:04.707 --> 00:43:06.334
you bring out plastic.

322
00:43:12.423 --> 00:43:13.758
We depend on fish.

323
00:43:15.843 --> 00:43:19.388
It's where we get
our resource from,

324
00:43:20.515 --> 00:43:24.894
to support our children to go
to school, to feed our family.

325
00:43:27.688 --> 00:43:28.981
It belongs to us.

326
00:43:38.908 --> 00:43:41.994
Without the ocean,
there's no community.

327
00:43:53.756 --> 00:43:56.008
When the trawlers take
away the last fish,

328
00:43:58.177 --> 00:43:59.554
my heart will be so heavy.

329
00:44:11.899 --> 00:44:14.652
Three billion people rely on

330
00:44:14.735 --> 00:44:16.487
our ocean for food.

331
00:44:18.614 --> 00:44:22.660
But ships sent by a few
wealthy nations are starving

332
00:44:22.743 --> 00:44:25.955
coastal communities of
the food source they have

333
00:44:26.038 --> 00:44:27.957
relied on for millennia.

334
00:44:30.918 --> 00:44:35.089
This is modern
colonialism at sea.

335
00:44:41.929 --> 00:44:46.809
Some 400,000
industrial vessels now hunt in

336
00:44:46.893 --> 00:44:48.728
every corner of the ocean.

337
00:44:54.942 --> 00:44:58.404
Nowhere is too far or too deep.

338
00:45:04.160 --> 00:45:08.331
Even the most remote parts of
the planet are no longer safe.

339
00:45:25.056 --> 00:45:30.019
Almost every animal in
the Antarctic relies on

340
00:45:30.102 --> 00:45:32.146
just a single species.

341
00:45:35.233 --> 00:45:37.693
A small red crustacean.

342
00:45:39.445 --> 00:45:41.030
Antarctic krill.

343
00:45:48.704 --> 00:45:51.207
For whales, fish, and penguins,

344
00:45:51.499 --> 00:45:54.460
krill is the most
important source of food

345
00:45:54.543 --> 00:45:56.337
throughout the
Southern Ocean.

346
00:46:42.425 --> 00:46:45.886
The trawlers have
reached Antarctica.

347
00:46:59.317 --> 00:47:02.278
And they are also
here for the krill.

348
00:47:08.409 --> 00:47:10.911
With so few fish
left in the ocean,

349
00:47:12.163 --> 00:47:15.833
we are now seeking
other prey in the furthest

350
00:47:15.916 --> 00:47:17.710
corners of our world.

351
00:47:27.887 --> 00:47:32.141
These are some of the
biggest factories on the sea.

352
00:47:37.938 --> 00:47:41.400
They suck hundreds of
thousands of tonnes of krill

353
00:47:41.817 --> 00:47:43.569
into vast nets.

354
00:47:55.414 --> 00:47:59.126
It's then boiled and
processed for fish farms,

355
00:48:00.419 --> 00:48:06.008
health supplements, and,
most recently, for pet food,

356
00:48:08.469 --> 00:48:10.346
all on board the ship.

357
00:48:34.245 --> 00:48:38.040
How can wildlife
compete with this?

358
00:48:54.682 --> 00:48:58.018
Some claim this is sustainable.

359
00:49:02.523 --> 00:49:06.110
But we may now be
removing the foundation of

360
00:49:06.193 --> 00:49:08.279
an entire ecosystem.

361
00:49:29.425 --> 00:49:32.595
We have drained the
life from our ocean.

362
00:49:35.973 --> 00:49:38.851
Now, we are almost out of time.

363
00:49:43.063 --> 00:49:47.276
Indeed, the ocean is in such
poor health that I would find

364
00:49:47.359 --> 00:49:52.781
it hard not to lose hope, were
it not for the most remarkable

365
00:49:52.865 --> 00:49:55.326
discovery of all.

366
00:49:58.287 --> 00:50:01.207
The ocean can
recover faster than we had

367
00:50:01.290 --> 00:50:02.583
ever thought possible.

368
00:50:03.083 --> 00:50:05.127
It can bounce back to life.

369
00:50:06.128 --> 00:50:09.131
For if left alone, it
may not just recover,

370
00:50:09.340 --> 00:50:13.010
but thrive beyond anything
anyone alive has ever seen.

371
00:50:14.553 --> 00:50:17.139
We can be sure of
this beyond any doubt,

372
00:50:17.473 --> 00:50:20.184
because it is already happening.

373
00:50:33.239 --> 00:50:36.700
For over 200 years,
this area was fished

374
00:50:36.784 --> 00:50:40.079
intensively with nets and traps.

375
00:50:49.171 --> 00:50:53.634
With their predators removed,
urchin numbers exploded.

376
00:50:57.012 --> 00:51:02.268
Soaring kelp forests were
transformed into a barren world.

377
00:51:03.727 --> 00:51:07.731
With little left to catch, the
decision was made to stop all

378
00:51:07.815 --> 00:51:11.777
fishing across nearly
three hundred square miles.

379
00:51:16.657 --> 00:51:21.579
It would be a marine
reserve or 'no-take zone'.

380
00:51:24.540 --> 00:51:28.377
Once protected, the predators
could begin to recover.

381
00:51:36.135 --> 00:51:37.845
Balance was restored.

382
00:51:40.889 --> 00:51:45.853
In just five years the forests
were once again flourishing.

383
00:51:48.939 --> 00:51:52.610
And with them, a thriving,
bustling neighborhood.

384
00:51:58.449 --> 00:52:03.287
Safe from fishing, the
animals had time to grow big.

385
00:52:11.295 --> 00:52:14.923
And the larger a female
spiny lobster can grow,

386
00:52:15.716 --> 00:52:18.302
the more eggs she can release.

387
00:52:31.273 --> 00:52:33.317
As the larvae disperse,

388
00:52:33.525 --> 00:52:36.737
they begin a journey
into the unknown.

389
00:52:43.118 --> 00:52:45.204
Leaving the safety of the kelp,

390
00:52:45.287 --> 00:52:48.624
many are carried by
currents into the open sea.

391
00:52:54.129 --> 00:52:57.633
Lobster larvae may travel
for thousands of miles,

392
00:52:58.050 --> 00:53:00.761
even crossing
international borders.

393
00:53:03.889 --> 00:53:06.642
They keep moving
for almost a year,

394
00:53:07.184 --> 00:53:09.603
developing and
growing along the way.

395
00:53:12.773 --> 00:53:14.942
Those that survive the journey,

396
00:53:15.734 --> 00:53:19.238
return to the coast to
begin their adult lives.

397
00:53:23.325 --> 00:53:27.454
And the chances are that
when they finally settle,

398
00:53:27.830 --> 00:53:31.375
they are outside
the no-take zone.

399
00:53:39.675 --> 00:53:45.472
This is how lobster populations
spill into unprotected waters,

400
00:53:45.889 --> 00:53:48.642
reviving fisheries far and wide.

401
00:54:00.154 --> 00:54:04.950
Initially it had
almost no effect whatsoever.

402
00:54:09.079 --> 00:54:12.624
And then the spillover
started to happen.

403
00:54:17.296 --> 00:54:20.758
Eventually, it turned
into something that was very

404
00:54:20.841 --> 00:54:21.842
hard to ignore.

405
00:54:26.096 --> 00:54:27.389
On one particular day,

406
00:54:27.514 --> 00:54:29.433
fishing on the edge
of the reserve,

407
00:54:30.184 --> 00:54:32.394
we pulled up the
trap of my career.

408
00:54:39.026 --> 00:54:40.736
The most I'd ever
seen in a trap.

409
00:54:41.612 --> 00:54:44.072
One for the record
books right there.

410
00:54:46.450 --> 00:54:48.911
It was an ecstatic moment,

411
00:54:49.161 --> 00:54:50.913
and it was an
eye-opening moment

412
00:54:56.627 --> 00:55:00.214
I think every fisherman I
know will acknowledge the fact

413
00:55:00.380 --> 00:55:02.925
that the reserves are working.

414
00:55:07.596 --> 00:55:13.519
It's an amazing thing to see
how abundant the species is.

415
00:55:14.353 --> 00:55:17.523
There's a bit of magic in there,
that's for certain, you know.

416
00:55:27.032 --> 00:55:30.077
The
'no-take zone' is literally

417
00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:32.871
overflowing with marine life.

418
00:55:45.801 --> 00:55:48.470
If we just let nature
take its course,

419
00:55:48.887 --> 00:55:50.847
the sea will save itself.

420
00:55:55.227 --> 00:55:58.313
Huge predators, not
seen for decades,

421
00:55:58.480 --> 00:56:00.232
have returned once again.

422
00:56:04.069 --> 00:56:09.032
Even giant black sea bass,
once assumed lost forever,

423
00:56:09.449 --> 00:56:12.452
now finds shelter
among the fronds.

424
00:56:38.937 --> 00:56:42.691
Recovery like this
is possible anywhere.

425
00:56:44.693 --> 00:56:48.864
Even in the most overfished
part of the most overfished

426
00:56:48.947 --> 00:56:50.407
sea on Earth.

427
00:56:53.493 --> 00:56:57.247
Fish populations in the
Mediterranean have almost

428
00:56:57.331 --> 00:56:58.749
completely collapsed,

429
00:57:02.794 --> 00:57:05.047
a third have already been lost.

430
00:57:06.798 --> 00:57:10.093
Yet even here there
is still hope.

431
00:57:24.107 --> 00:57:26.985
When a tiny reserve
was established off the

432
00:57:27.069 --> 00:57:32.449
coast of France,
it erupted back to life.

433
00:57:44.169 --> 00:57:47.130
Even the biggest fish
are making a comeback.

434
00:58:00.852 --> 00:58:06.024
This is the result of fully
protecting less than 1%

435
00:58:06.191 --> 00:58:07.651
of the Mediterranean.

436
00:58:11.863 --> 00:58:14.157
But this is just the beginning.

437
00:58:15.784 --> 00:58:19.329
If protecting a small portion
of the sea from fishing has

438
00:58:19.413 --> 00:58:23.250
such a large effect, imagine
the potential power of doing

439
00:58:23.333 --> 00:58:25.711
this across much larger areas.

440
00:58:26.461 --> 00:58:29.381
Now for the first
time this century,

441
00:58:29.464 --> 00:58:33.760
we don't need to imagine,
we can see for ourselves.

442
00:59:04.458 --> 00:59:06.960
It is literally
a place of our ancestors.

443
00:59:09.421 --> 00:59:11.548
What we would
call a "wao akua."

444
00:59:13.258 --> 00:59:15.010
a realm of the gods.

445
00:59:19.055 --> 00:59:22.058
Papahanaumokuakea is the
largest fully protected area,

446
00:59:22.142 --> 00:59:24.102
land or sea, on the planet.

447
00:59:30.817 --> 00:59:34.070
Those calls for protection came
from native Hawai'ian fishermen

448
00:59:34.154 --> 00:59:35.197
and elders.

449
00:59:37.491 --> 00:59:41.411
Without it, there would be
no moli, no Laysan albatross.

450
00:59:48.502 --> 00:59:52.714
Their home is the entirety
of the vast Pacific Ocean.

451
00:59:57.969 --> 01:00:02.432
They are seers of the sky.

452
01:00:07.062 --> 01:00:10.357
They see more of the planet
than almost any creature.

453
01:00:14.110 --> 01:00:18.698
But they depend on tiny little
spits of land in order to breed.

454
01:00:24.079 --> 01:00:27.040
Those of us who come from
voyaging and way-finding

455
01:00:27.123 --> 01:00:30.085
traditions could only
watch them in wonder.

456
01:00:37.259 --> 01:00:40.178
Over years, each pair
develops their own language.

457
01:00:42.931 --> 01:00:45.642
By dancing together they
reaffirm their bond.

458
01:01:06.705 --> 01:01:11.209
Moli are the longest
living known species of bird

459
01:01:11.293 --> 01:01:12.168
on the planet.

460
01:01:14.421 --> 01:01:17.716
It's hard not to look at
this moli chick and wonder

461
01:01:18.174 --> 01:01:20.844
"What will it see,
in the next 60 years?"

462
01:01:30.186 --> 01:01:32.981
Thanks to the vision of the
elders and the Hawai'ian

463
01:01:33.064 --> 01:01:36.276
fishermen who first
charged us with taking

464
01:01:36.359 --> 01:01:38.737
better care of this place,

465
01:01:40.947 --> 01:01:43.241
they have gone from
near extinction to the largest

466
01:01:43.325 --> 01:01:45.702
albatross colony in the world.

467
01:01:50.415 --> 01:01:54.085
Now, 14 million seabirds
return each year.

468
01:01:56.671 --> 01:02:01.509
It is a pu'uhonua,
a place of refuge.

469
01:02:04.971 --> 01:02:07.974
Why it's important to protect
really big areas is for

470
01:02:08.058 --> 01:02:11.478
migratory species to be
the voyagers that they are.

471
01:02:13.146 --> 01:02:14.272
They need a lot of space.

472
01:02:33.166 --> 01:02:34.584
People said it couldn't be done.

473
01:02:35.543 --> 01:02:38.630
But due to the protections
of Papahanaumokuakea,

474
01:02:39.047 --> 01:02:42.759
tuna can reproduce and spread
into neighboring areas,

475
01:02:43.385 --> 01:02:46.846
where populations
have increased by 54%.

476
01:03:02.570 --> 01:03:04.406
Spillover is basic biology.

477
01:03:06.992 --> 01:03:10.203
When we first set out to
protect what is now

478
01:03:10.286 --> 01:03:12.998
Papahanaumokuakea,
the people would ask us,

479
01:03:13.081 --> 01:03:15.250
"What are you
protecting it from?"

480
01:03:16.042 --> 01:03:17.419
and our answer was,

481
01:03:17.502 --> 01:03:19.295
"That's the wrong question.

482
01:03:19.379 --> 01:03:23.591
It's about who and what
are we protecting it for?"

483
01:03:29.180 --> 01:03:33.101
Protecting the ocean doesn't
mean being anti-fishing.

484
01:03:34.102 --> 01:03:38.648
The goals of healthy fisheries
and conservation are the same,

485
01:03:39.482 --> 01:03:43.653
more fish, more abundance,
more health of the ocean.

486
01:04:03.840 --> 01:04:07.594
-Until now, it was assumed
that no protected area could

487
01:04:07.677 --> 01:04:11.222
be big enough to help
fish species that migrate

488
01:04:11.306 --> 01:04:14.517
over long distances,
such as yellowfin tuna.

489
01:04:15.226 --> 01:04:18.313
This place has shattered
that assumption.

490
01:04:21.357 --> 01:04:24.277
For when we create a
nature reserve on land,

491
01:04:24.527 --> 01:04:26.863
we mostly protect
the life within it.

492
01:04:28.198 --> 01:04:30.825
But we have discovered
that when you do the same

493
01:04:30.909 --> 01:04:34.454
in the ocean, the
effects of these reserves

494
01:04:34.537 --> 01:04:37.290
spread far beyond
their boundaries.

495
01:04:38.792 --> 01:04:43.338
The waters outside
fill with life again.

496
01:04:57.811 --> 01:05:01.147
Wherever we have given
the ocean time and space,

497
01:05:01.898 --> 01:05:06.027
it has recovered fast and
on a greater scale than we

498
01:05:06.111 --> 01:05:08.488
dared to imagine possible.

499
01:05:15.703 --> 01:05:18.706
And it has the power
to go even further,

500
01:05:19.499 --> 01:05:23.711
to defend against the
greatest threats of our time.

501
01:05:31.177 --> 01:05:33.721
When corals experience
heat stress,

502
01:05:34.389 --> 01:05:39.227
they evict their plant partners
and turn ghostly white.

503
01:05:44.983 --> 01:05:48.486
During filming, the
temperature of our ocean rose

504
01:05:48.570 --> 01:05:50.738
higher than ever before.

505
01:05:54.868 --> 01:05:58.454
A devastating heat wave
travelled across the planet,

506
01:06:02.000 --> 01:06:04.377
transforming even
the largest and most

507
01:06:04.502 --> 01:06:06.713
established tropical reefs.

508
01:06:16.973 --> 01:06:20.018
As far as we know,
these reefs have never looked

509
01:06:20.101 --> 01:06:21.811
like this before.

510
01:06:35.408 --> 01:06:39.037
These Elkhorn corals
in the Caribbean...

511
01:06:41.414 --> 01:06:43.249
...were some of the last.

512
01:06:53.051 --> 01:06:57.388
We are likely here to be
witnessing the extinction of

513
01:06:57.472 --> 01:07:00.808
species before our very eyes.

514
01:07:13.154 --> 01:07:16.407
This was the biggest
global mass bleaching event

515
01:07:16.616 --> 01:07:18.201
in recorded history.

516
01:07:25.250 --> 01:07:29.712
If the warming continues,
the corals never recover.

517
01:07:31.422 --> 01:07:36.010
They're smothered
by algae... and die.

518
01:07:39.180 --> 01:07:42.934
Nearly all coral reefs are
predicted to disappear in the

519
01:07:43.017 --> 01:07:45.103
next 30 years.

520
01:07:49.065 --> 01:07:52.151
You could be forgiven for
thinking there is no hope

521
01:07:52.235 --> 01:07:53.319
left at all.

522
01:07:56.948 --> 01:07:58.032
But there is.

523
01:08:01.828 --> 01:08:07.625
In just a few places,
strict protection from fishing

524
01:08:08.543 --> 01:08:11.713
has allowed armies of
grazers to thrive.

525
01:08:14.632 --> 01:08:17.635
They hold back the
smothering algae,

526
01:08:18.386 --> 01:08:20.722
giving the corals a
chance to recover.

527
01:08:24.434 --> 01:08:26.978
These are baby corals,

528
01:08:27.812 --> 01:08:30.732
new life emerging
from the wreckage.

529
01:08:34.444 --> 01:08:35.862
In some places,

530
01:08:36.195 --> 01:08:38.948
entire reefs have
come back to life.

531
01:08:57.550 --> 01:09:01.846
Protection from fishing has
made these the most resilient

532
01:09:01.929 --> 01:09:03.806
reefs on the planet.

533
01:09:18.196 --> 01:09:21.491
This is the power of protection.

534
01:09:30.124 --> 01:09:34.837
It is the same story in
every ocean of the world.

535
01:09:49.143 --> 01:09:53.022
If I asked you, "How much of
the ocean is truly protected?"

536
01:09:53.439 --> 01:09:55.900
What would you say, 20%?

537
01:09:56.442 --> 01:09:58.194
At least ten, surely?

538
01:09:58.986 --> 01:10:02.865
The answer is less than 3%,

539
01:10:03.324 --> 01:10:06.285
in practical terms, none of it.

540
01:10:09.372 --> 01:10:12.792
Many of the Marine Protected
Areas that do exist are

541
01:10:12.875 --> 01:10:16.462
exploited using destructive
forms of fishing,

542
01:10:16.546 --> 01:10:18.673
and are barely safe at all.

543
01:10:22.218 --> 01:10:25.555
To save it from collapse,
scientists have said we must

544
01:10:25.638 --> 01:10:28.433
fully protect at least a third.

545
01:10:29.183 --> 01:10:32.019
For it to work it should
be, for the most part,

546
01:10:32.103 --> 01:10:34.939
along our heavily
fished coastlines.

547
01:10:45.783 --> 01:10:48.911
And the area we choose
should also include those

548
01:10:48.995 --> 01:10:54.625
magnets for life out in
the open ocean, seamounts.

549
01:11:01.716 --> 01:11:04.010
If we can protect
areas on land,

550
01:11:04.093 --> 01:11:06.763
where the entire human
population requires

551
01:11:06.846 --> 01:11:08.431
space to live,

552
01:11:08.973 --> 01:11:11.476
surely we can
do it in the sea.

553
01:11:15.563 --> 01:11:19.400
Restoring the ocean is
for everyone on Earth.

554
01:11:26.657 --> 01:11:30.411
In front of us is a
chance to protect our climate,

555
01:11:30.703 --> 01:11:32.914
our food, our home.

556
01:11:35.082 --> 01:11:37.418
After a lifetime of
filming the natural world,

557
01:11:37.502 --> 01:11:40.671
I cannot remember a
more exciting opportunity

558
01:11:40.755 --> 01:11:42.256
for our species.

559
01:11:48.846 --> 01:11:51.474
This could be the
moment of change.

560
01:11:52.517 --> 01:11:56.395
Nearly every country on Earth
has just agreed, on paper,

561
01:11:56.479 --> 01:11:59.273
to achieve this bare
minimum and fully protect a

562
01:11:59.357 --> 01:12:01.484
third of the ocean.

563
01:12:03.694 --> 01:12:07.907
Together, we now face the
challenge of making it happen.

564
01:12:12.787 --> 01:12:15.039
Without the ocean, simply put,

565
01:12:15.122 --> 01:12:16.415
we wouldn't exist.

566
01:12:18.709 --> 01:12:20.169
The planet will go on.

567
01:12:20.253 --> 01:12:22.630
The question is,
will we be part of it?

568
01:12:25.508 --> 01:12:28.553
I like to
compare our fisheries to having

569
01:12:28.636 --> 01:12:30.388
an apple tree in the backyard.

570
01:12:31.472 --> 01:12:34.058
Do you want to pick all
your apples in one day?

571
01:12:35.393 --> 01:12:38.813
No, you want to go out and
you want to pick a few apples.

572
01:12:38.896 --> 01:12:40.481
You want to take what you need.

573
01:12:43.693 --> 01:12:45.903
I'm not
saying that people shouldn't

574
01:12:45.987 --> 01:12:48.364
catch fish, eat fish.

575
01:12:49.323 --> 01:12:51.659
And fishermen should be
able to make a living.

576
01:12:51.993 --> 01:12:56.414
But they shouldn't be
able to do it in a way that

577
01:12:56.497 --> 01:12:59.584
is just breaking everything.

578
01:13:02.795 --> 01:13:06.215
Our
community survives because

579
01:13:06.299 --> 01:13:07.216
of the ocean.

580
01:13:09.427 --> 01:13:13.347
The ocean gives life,
the ocean gives life.

581
01:13:16.142 --> 01:13:19.020
-Success on a
global scale is possible.

582
01:13:19.478 --> 01:13:22.398
I've seen it myself once before.

583
01:13:30.656 --> 01:13:32.533
The pattern was familiar.

584
01:13:35.453 --> 01:13:38.164
Once whaling became
industrialized,

585
01:13:43.210 --> 01:13:48.257
it took just 100 years
until nearly all species were

586
01:13:48.341 --> 01:13:51.218
pushed to the edge
of extinction.

587
01:13:55.765 --> 01:13:58.517
The blues, the
largest of them all,

588
01:13:58.684 --> 01:14:02.271
were pursued
until only 1% remained.

589
01:14:05.358 --> 01:14:11.697
I remember thinking that was it,
there was no coming back.

590
01:14:12.782 --> 01:14:14.951
We had lost the great whales.

591
01:14:19.080 --> 01:14:24.210
Finally, lawmakers acted
on public pressure to ban

592
01:14:24.293 --> 01:14:25.586
commercial whaling.

593
01:14:27.546 --> 01:14:31.550
What followed was beyond
our wildest dreams.

594
01:14:38.432 --> 01:14:43.813
The number of humpback whales
in the South Atlantic tripled

595
01:14:43.896 --> 01:14:45.940
in just ten years.

596
01:14:56.283 --> 01:15:01.455
And scenes like these of fin
whales in Antarctica were

597
01:15:01.539 --> 01:15:04.500
unimaginable just 50 years ago.

598
01:15:16.804 --> 01:15:19.306
Even the greatest
of our ocean giants,

599
01:15:19.390 --> 01:15:20.766
the blue whale,

600
01:15:21.851 --> 01:15:24.353
hunted to the very
edge of extinction,

601
01:15:24.437 --> 01:15:26.397
has begun to return.

602
01:15:31.235 --> 01:15:35.156
I would never have imaged
that in just over a decade of

603
01:15:35.239 --> 01:15:39.660
whaling being banned, I
could encounter one myself.

604
01:15:41.037 --> 01:15:44.457
I can see its tail
just under my boat here

605
01:15:45.041 --> 01:15:46.834
And it’s coming up,
it’s coming up!

606
01:15:47.543 --> 01:15:48.669
There!

607
01:15:49.587 --> 01:15:55.176
Only in the sea can you
get such huge size as that

608
01:15:55.259 --> 01:15:57.053
magnificent creature.

609
01:16:03.350 --> 01:16:07.063
Now we are forging a
new relationship with

610
01:16:07.146 --> 01:16:08.981
these gentle creatures.

611
01:16:20.743 --> 01:16:22.995
A blue whale born today...

612
01:16:26.290 --> 01:16:29.585
...could live
for over 100 years.

613
01:16:37.009 --> 01:16:41.972
If we apply the same foresight
to save her home as we once did

614
01:16:42.056 --> 01:16:46.852
to save her species,
then she will witness

615
01:16:46.936 --> 01:16:48.813
a remarkable recovery.

616
01:17:11.794 --> 01:17:13.629
Through the course of my life,

617
01:17:14.046 --> 01:17:17.383
we have been on a
voyage of ocean discovery.

618
01:17:20.052 --> 01:17:22.096
And what a journey it has been.

619
01:17:28.519 --> 01:17:31.397
When I first saw the
sea as a young boy,

620
01:17:31.480 --> 01:17:35.609
it was thought of as a vast
wilderness to be tamed and

621
01:17:35.693 --> 01:17:38.279
mastered for the
benefit of humanity.

622
01:17:39.363 --> 01:17:42.158
Now, as I approach
the end of my life,

623
01:17:42.616 --> 01:17:44.743
we know the opposite is true.

624
01:17:49.498 --> 01:17:54.795
It is my great hope that we
all come to see the ocean not

625
01:17:54.879 --> 01:17:58.632
as a dark and distant
place with little relevance

626
01:17:58.716 --> 01:18:00.342
to our lives on land,

627
01:18:00.843 --> 01:18:04.346
but as the
lifeblood of our home.

628
01:18:07.683 --> 01:18:11.145
I'm sure that nothing
is more important.

629
01:18:16.358 --> 01:18:19.069
For if we save the sea...

630
01:18:23.991 --> 01:18:27.036
...we save our world.

631
